# Line endings are checked out LF everywhere, and that is a correctness requirement here rather than a
# preference.
#
# `Bearing.Sql.SqlFormatWriter` deliberately *preserves* the line endings of the SQL it is given, defaulting
# to LF when the input has none (SqlFormatWriter.cs) — which is right for a formatter working on a buffer it
# did not create. `SqlFormatTests` therefore compares its output against raw string literals in the test
# file, so **the expected value depends on how the file was checked out**: a single-line input formats to LF
# output, and on a CRLF checkout the multi-line expected literal beside it is CRLF. Forty of those tests
# passed on every machine here (git config core.autocrlf=false) and failed the first time CI ran them on a
# Windows runner, where git's default autocrlf=true converts on checkout.
#
# Normalising is the fix rather than loosening the assertions: a test that ignored line endings would stop
# catching the thing the formatter is careful about.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF regardless — a CRLF shebang line makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose
# name ends in \r, and the script fails with "bad interpreter".
*.sh text eol=lf

# Binaries, so nothing tries to normalise them.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary
